WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
Access models should come from business rules before they become code.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:12.840
A team should be able to explain who needs access, which operations they perform, which resources they affect, and what conditions limit that access.

3
00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:18.620
Framework convenience can help implementation, but it should not define the policy by accident.

4
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:29.440
If the business rule is that project members can update their own project tasks but only billing administrators can export invoices, the code should express that distinction clearly.

5
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:41.720
Common building blocks include roles, permissions, groups, scopes, claims, ownership, tenant membership, team membership, and relationships.

6
00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.220
A role might represent a job function.

7
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.360
A permission might represent a specific action.

8
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.380
A group might aggregate users.

9
00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:55.000
A scope or claim may carry authorization-relevant information in an identity or token workflow.

10
00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:05.180
Ownership and relationship-based access at a high level answer questions such as whether this user belongs to this project, manages this team, or owns this resource.

11
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:15.220
Role-based access control, attribute-based access control, relationship-based access control, and policy-based access concepts are design tools.

12
00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.960
RBAC is understandable and useful, but it can become too broad or too fragmented.

13
00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:29.100
Attribute-based rules can express context, such as tenant, department, sensitivity, or workflow state, but require good data quality.

14
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.600
Relationship-based rules are powerful for collaborative applications.

15
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:39.540
Policy-based approaches can improve consistency when rules need to be shared across services.

16
00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.500
Least privilege and default deny are the safest baseline.

17
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:51.280
Access should be denied unless a clear policy allows it, and permissions should be no broader than the role, task, or service needs.

18
00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:56.260
The model should be understandable to humans and precise enough for software enforcement.

19
00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:05.320
If nobody can explain why a permission exists, who owns it, how it is reviewed, and which actions it allows, the model will be hard to operate safely.
